  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "11561872",
      "title": "Understanding Spread & Rest Operators",
      "lectureOrder": 18,
      "videoOrder": 16,
      "sourceHash": "5885fa6b19ca40781e97e4e6020b55d474c706b67eaf1574d6d16c7b5db84f7c",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 2.56,
          "end": 9.82,
          "en": "In modern JavaScript, there are two important operators you also should be aware of, since I'll be",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 9.82,
          "end": 15.16,
          "en": "using them throughout the course as well, and that are the rest and spread operators.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 15.19,
          "end": 19.18,
          "en": "Now specifically, the spread operator is one we'll use quite a bit.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 19.42,
          "end": 26.38,
          "en": "Let's say we want to implement the pattern where when we add a new hobby, we don't edit the original",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 26.38,
          "end": 32.71,
          "en": "array, but we create a new array with all the old values and the new value.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 32.74,
          "end": 38.86,
          "en": "This is actually a pretty common pattern called immutability, where we never added existing values,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 38.86,
          "end": 43.09,
          "en": "but where we always replace them with copies plus the changes.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 43.09,
          "end": 45.97,
          "en": "And that is all the pattern I'll use quite a lot in the course.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 46.24,
          "end": 53.17,
          "en": "The idea behind that is that we avoid errors because we always have this clear approach of copy, then",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 53.17,
          "end": 58.45,
          "en": "add it and don't edit existing objects which might lead to more unreadable code.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 58.72,
          "end": 63.88,
          "en": "Now to copy an array, let's say here I create a copied array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 63.88,
          "end": 66.46,
          "en": "We got a couple of possible techniques.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 66.46,
          "end": 68.95,
          "en": "One of them is to use the slice operator.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 68.98,
          "end": 75.28,
          "en": "Now if I output copy the arrays down there and I run node pages, I see sports and cooking.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 75.28,
          "end": 76.75,
          "en": "So I did indeed copy it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 76.78,
          "end": 79.59,
          "en": "Slice simply copies an array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 79.6,
          "end": 83.56,
          "en": "We can pass arguments to narrow down the range of elements we want to copy.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 83.56,
          "end": 84.82,
          "en": "With no arguments.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 84.82,
          "end": 86.44,
          "en": "We copy the entire array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 88.3,
          "end": 91.9,
          "en": "Now instead of slides, there is also a different technique.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 92.02000000000001,
          "end": 96.74000000000001,
          "en": "We can create a new array with square brackets and we could add hobby stair.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 96.75999999999999,
          "end": 97.36,
          "en": "Right.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 97.39,
          "end": 99.88,
          "en": "Now what happens if we execute this?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 99.88,
          "end": 101.92,
          "en": "What will we see in the console?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 102.6,
          "end": 105.19,
          "en": "Well, if I hit enter, we see.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 105.21000000000001,
          "end": 111.47,
          "en": "Well, it looks like a copy on first sight, but actually it's an array with a number array in it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 111.47999999999999,
          "end": 116.09,
          "en": "So the outer array has only one element and that's the inner array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 116.1,
          "end": 121.44,
          "en": "So it's not a copy, it's just a new array where the first element is the old array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 121.44,
          "end": 125.25,
          "en": "And with that I mean the exact same object, not a copy of that.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 125.79,
          "end": 127.74,
          "en": "So we just created a.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 128.82,
          "end": 130.14,
          "en": "Nested array here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 130.17,
          "end": 133.8,
          "en": "That, of course, was not what we did want to do here and here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 133.8,
          "end": 136.11,
          "en": "We can use the spread operator.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 136.11,
          "end": 138.6,
          "en": "The spread operator are three dots.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 138.6,
          "end": 142.07999999999998,
          "en": "We can add in front of an array or of an object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 142.44,
          "end": 147.24,
          "en": "And these three dots are an operator, just as the plus or minus are.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 147.87,
          "end": 149.46,
          "en": "And they do one thing.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 149.97,
          "end": 159.75,
          "en": "They take the array or object after the operator and pull out all the elements or properties.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 159.75,
          "end": 168.9,
          "en": "So all the elements of an array are all the properties of an object and put it to whatever is around",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 168.9,
          "end": 169.62,
          "en": "that spread.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 169.62,
          "end": 170.31,
          "en": "Operator.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 172.04,
          "end": 177.98,
          "en": "In this case, we got square brackets around the spread operator and therefore all the elements which",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 177.98,
          "end": 183.02,
          "en": "are pulled out of the existing array are added to the new array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 183.32,
          "end": 189.5,
          "en": "And therefore, if I now run this file again, whoops, and I save it before running it again, now",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 189.5,
          "end": 192.92,
          "en": "we see this was the output of the old approach, the nested array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 192.95,
          "end": 195.32,
          "en": "Now we got no nested array anymore.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 195.32,
          "end": 203.03,
          "en": "We got one array and this is now a copy of the old one because we take the spread operator to pull out",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 203.03,
          "end": 207.86,
          "en": "these elements and add them one by one to the new array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 208.31,
          "end": 213.8,
          "en": "So this is something you'll see we do a lot to copy existing arrays or objects there.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 213.8,
          "end": 221.75,
          "en": "It would work in the same way we could have our copied person by using curly braces, then the spread",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 221.75,
          "end": 225.23,
          "en": "operator, the three dots, and then the old person.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 225.53,
          "end": 228.56,
          "en": "And now if I console.log, the copied person here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 230.52,
          "end": 232.84,
          "en": "And I execute this file again.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 232.86,
          "end": 239.64,
          "en": "This is our copied person here because I'm pulling out all these elements from that object and I add",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 239.64,
          "end": 240.78,
          "en": "it to a new object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 240.78,
          "end": 247.5,
          "en": "So this works for both objects and arrays, and it is a syntax I'll use quite a bit in this course.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 247.74,
          "end": 249.87,
          "en": "Now this is the spread operator.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 251.56,
          "end": 258.22,
          "en": "I also mentioned the rest operator, and the rest operator is essentially the opposite.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 258.99,
          "end": 262.68,
          "en": "Let's say I have a function which I'll name to array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 263.16,
          "end": 264.42,
          "en": "It's an arrow function.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 264.78,
          "end": 270.24,
          "en": "And there I expect argument's arc one arc to arc three.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 271.03,
          "end": 274.15,
          "en": "I want to return an array that contains these arguments.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 274.18,
          "end": 274.59000000000003,
          "en": "Whoops.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 274.6,
          "end": 276.61,
          "en": "And I should add a equals sign here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 276.61,
          "end": 279.97,
          "en": "So I want to return an array that contains these arguments.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 280.3,
          "end": 282.90999999999997,
          "en": "I can return square brackets here, of course.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 282.90999999999997,
          "end": 285.28,
          "en": "And then the first element will be ARG one.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 68,
          "start": 285.7,
          "end": 289.78,
          "en": "Then I have ARG two as the second element and arg three as the first element.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "start": 290.83,
          "end": 301.42,
          "en": "Now I can console lock to array and I pass one, two and three as the free arguments to that function.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "start": 302.17,
          "end": 306.97,
          "en": "If I now execute pledges, we see an array with one, two and three, these three elements.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 71,
          "start": 306.97,
          "end": 310.58,
          "en": "So this is working, but this is totally not flexible.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "start": 310.6,
          "end": 312.91,
          "en": "What if we want to pass four arguments?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "start": 312.94,
          "end": 314.69,
          "en": "Well, we could call it like that.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 74,
          "start": 314.71,
          "end": 319.96,
          "en": "JavaScript actually allows that, but of course it doesn't get added because we only work with three",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 75,
          "start": 319.96,
          "end": 320.89,
          "en": "arguments here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 76,
          "start": 321.16,
          "end": 324.82,
          "en": "What we could do is we could use the so called rest operator there.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 77,
          "start": 327.82,
          "end": 330.46,
          "en": "Dot, dot, dot and then just arcs.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 78,
          "start": 330.58,
          "end": 333.37,
          "en": "And this will actually take all the arguments.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 79,
          "start": 333.37,
          "end": 335.13,
          "en": "How many we might specify.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 80,
          "start": 335.14,
          "end": 339.49,
          "en": "That doesn't matter and it will bundle them up in an array for us.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 81,
          "start": 339.49,
          "end": 343.39,
          "en": "So here args will be an array and I can just return that actually.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 82,
          "start": 343.84000000000003,
          "end": 352.39,
          "en": "And now if I re execute this with two array getting four arguments, you see now I have my array with",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 83,
          "start": 352.39,
          "end": 353.53,
          "en": "four arguments here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 84,
          "start": 353.62,
          "end": 355.87,
          "en": "So the rest operator looks just like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 85,
          "start": 355.87,
          "end": 357.76,
          "en": "Bread operator three dots.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 86,
          "start": 357.76,
          "end": 361.54,
          "en": "And it's the place where you use it that defines how you call it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 87,
          "start": 361.81,
          "end": 368.5,
          "en": "Are you using it to pull elements or properties out of arrays or objects?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 88,
          "start": 368.5,
          "end": 371.02,
          "en": "Then it would be the spread operator.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 89,
          "start": 371.53,
          "end": 380.41,
          "en": "Are you using it to merge multiple arguments into an array and you use it in the argument list of a",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 90,
          "start": 380.41,
          "end": 381.25,
          "en": "function.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 91,
          "start": 381.25,
          "end": 383.83,
          "en": "Then it's the rest operator.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 92,
          "start": 383.83,
          "end": 389.95,
          "en": "It's the same operator by the syntax or from a syntax perspective, the name differs depending on the",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 93,
          "start": 389.95,
          "end": 391.48,
          "en": "place where you use it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 94,
          "start": 391.75,
          "end": 395.53,
          "en": "I'll not use that syntax a lot in this course, but it's still nice to know.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 95,
          "start": 395.53,
          "end": 401.02,
          "en": "But being able to pull out elements or properties, that is something you should understand because",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 96,
          "start": 401.02,
          "end": 402.01,
          "en": "that is a syntax.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 97,
          "start": 402.01,
          "end": 404.38,
          "en": "You'll see me use quite a bit throughout the course.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
